1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:02,556
On November tenth, twenty twenty five,

2
00:00:02,606 --> 00:00:08,606
Ecuador moved three hundred of its most dangerous prisoners into a brand new maximum security prison.

3
00:00:08,656 --> 00:00:11,660
There was one problem. The prison was not finished.

4
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:17,220
Two days later, the president himself admitted it. The building, he said,

5
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:19,963
was only thirty five to forty percent complete.

6
00:00:21,313 --> 00:00:22,317
So why the rush?

7
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:26,273
Why put three hundred gang leaders into a construction site?

8
00:00:26,323 --> 00:00:28,580
And what has happened inside since?

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:32,740
Chapter 1. A country at war with its prisons.

10
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:35,567
For decades,

11
00:00:35,617 --> 00:00:39,677
Ecuador was known as one of the calmer countries in South America.

12
00:00:39,727 --> 00:00:42,728
Then, in the late twenty tens, that changed fast.

13
00:00:43,478 --> 00:00:45,733
Ecuador sits between Colombia and Peru,

14
00:00:45,783 --> 00:00:49,634
the two biggest cocaine producers in the world. Its Pacific ports,

15
00:00:49,684 --> 00:00:50,875
especially Guayaquil,

16
00:00:50,925 --> 00:00:54,835
became a highway for drugs heading to the United States and Europe.

17
00:00:54,885 --> 00:00:57,554
And local gangs, working with foreign cartels,

18
00:00:57,604 --> 00:00:59,859
began fighting to control that highway.

19
00:01:00,609 --> 00:01:03,063
Much of that war was run from prison.

20
00:01:03,113 --> 00:01:07,260
Gangs like Los Choneros and Los Lobos controlled entire wings.

21
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:11,727
Leaders gave orders by phone. And when rival gangs clashed inside,

22
00:01:11,777 --> 00:01:13,554
the results were massacres.

23
00:01:14,304 --> 00:01:18,443
In September twenty twenty one, at the Litoral Penitentiary in Guayaquil,

24
00:01:18,493 --> 00:01:23,033
more than one hundred and sixteen prisoners were killed in a single gang battle.

25
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:24,984
Some of the victims were beheaded.

26
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:28,828
It was one of the worst prison massacres in Latin American history.

27
00:01:29,578 --> 00:01:33,123
And it was not the only one. According to Ecuadorian media,

28
00:01:33,173 --> 00:01:38,243
around six hundred prisoners have died in Ecuador's prisons since twenty twenty one,

29
00:01:38,293 --> 00:01:39,827
most of them in massacres.

30
00:01:40,577 --> 00:01:44,810
Noboa later described the prisons his government inherited in one sentence.

31
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:49,265
They are prisons, he said, that we received completely destroyed. The problem,

32
00:01:49,315 --> 00:01:51,321
he added, was not only overcrowding.

33
00:01:51,371 --> 00:01:53,662
Many prisons had simply never been fixed.

34
00:01:54,632 --> 00:01:57,103
Chapter 2. The Bukele promise.

35
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:01,164
In November twenty twenty three,

36
00:02:01,214 --> 00:02:05,311
a young businessman named Daniel Noboa became president of Ecuador.

37
00:02:05,361 --> 00:02:06,487
He was thirty five.

38
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:10,597
In January twenty twenty four, the country exploded.

39
00:02:10,647 --> 00:02:12,892
Gang violence spread across cities.

40
00:02:12,942 --> 00:02:17,089
Armed men stormed a live television studio in Guayaquil, on air.

41
00:02:17,139 --> 00:02:20,762
Noboa responded by declaring an internal armed conflict,

42
00:02:20,812 --> 00:02:23,909
and naming the gangs as terrorist organizations.

43
00:02:24,659 --> 00:02:28,521
And he made a promise that sounded very familiar. Ecuador,

44
00:02:28,571 --> 00:02:33,444
like El Salvador, would build new high security prisons. Prisons modeled,

45
00:02:33,494 --> 00:02:35,804
many said, on Nayib Bukele's CECOT.

46
00:02:36,554 --> 00:02:38,810
On January eleventh, twenty twenty four,

47
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:41,981
Noboa presented the designs in the presidential palace.

48
00:02:42,031 --> 00:02:46,420
One of the new prisons would be built in the coastal province of Santa Elena.

49
00:02:47,170 --> 00:02:50,222
Its name would be El Encuentro. The Encounter.

50
00:02:51,772 --> 00:02:56,292
A second prison was planned in the Amazon region. After local protests,

51
00:02:56,342 --> 00:03:00,606
that project was moved to another site. But Santa Elena went first.

52
00:03:01,576 --> 00:03:04,035
Chapter 3. Three hundred days.

53
00:03:06,165 --> 00:03:11,454
The site was chosen in a remote area near the village of Juntas del Pacifico.

54
00:03:11,504 --> 00:03:16,446
Dry forest. An unpaved road that becomes impassable in the rainy season.

55
00:03:16,496 --> 00:03:22,062
According to Ecuadorian reporting, no drinking water, no sewage, and no internet.

56
00:03:22,112 --> 00:03:24,836
The prison would have to supply its own.

57
00:03:25,586 --> 00:03:27,142
Local communities protested.

58
00:03:27,192 --> 00:03:30,815
They did not want a maximum security prison next to their homes.

59
00:03:30,865 --> 00:03:32,307
The government went ahead.

60
00:03:33,057 --> 00:03:35,520
On June twenty first, twenty twenty four,

61
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:38,156
Noboa led a ceremony to start construction.

62
00:03:38,206 --> 00:03:41,404
The cost was put at around fifty two million dollars.

63
00:03:41,454 --> 00:03:44,408
The builder was a Spanish company group, Puentes,

64
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:47,534
controlled by a Chinese state construction company.

65
00:03:48,284 --> 00:03:51,704
The head of Ecuador's prison agency made a bold promise.

66
00:03:51,754 --> 00:03:55,299
The prison would be built in three hundred days. A record,

67
00:03:55,349 --> 00:03:55,795
he said.

68
00:03:56,545 --> 00:04:01,160
The plans were impressive. Thirty seven hectares. A nine meter wall,

69
00:04:01,210 --> 00:04:05,757
six meters of concrete topped with three meters of anti climb mesh.

70
00:04:05,807 --> 00:04:10,972
Six watchtowers, nine and a half meters high. Five maximum security modules.

71
00:04:11,022 --> 00:04:15,843
Room for about eight hundred prisoners. Iris scanners. Signal blockers.

72
00:04:15,893 --> 00:04:19,342
Cameras connected to the national emergency system.

73
00:04:20,092 --> 00:04:23,847
In October twenty twenty four, Noboa flew in to inspect the work.

74
00:04:23,897 --> 00:04:25,778
It was about thirty percent done.

75
00:04:25,828 --> 00:04:29,232
The photos from that visit show bare concrete, steel rebar,

76
00:04:29,282 --> 00:04:31,690
and scaffolding where the towers would be.

77
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,340
Three hundred days passed. The prison was not ready.

78
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:39,163
Chapter 4. Five weeks.

79
00:04:41,293 --> 00:04:44,814
By September twenty twenty five, the deadline had long gone.

80
00:04:44,864 --> 00:04:47,968
On September twenty sixth, in a television interview,

81
00:04:48,018 --> 00:04:51,659
Noboa said the prison would be finished in four to five weeks.

82
00:04:52,409 --> 00:04:53,475
At the same time,

83
00:04:53,525 --> 00:04:58,532
the violence inside Ecuador's old prisons had not stopped. In late September,

84
00:04:58,582 --> 00:05:02,670
eleven prisoners were killed in a clash in Machala. Days later,

85
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:04,575
seventeen died in Esmeraldas.

86
00:05:05,325 --> 00:05:09,223
And there was something else on the calendar. On November sixteenth,

87
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:10,326
twenty twenty five,

88
00:05:10,376 --> 00:05:14,332
Ecuadorians would vote in a national referendum on Noboa's proposals.

89
00:05:14,382 --> 00:05:17,235
Including whether to allow foreign military bases,

90
00:05:17,285 --> 00:05:19,558
and whether to write a new constitution.

91
00:05:20,308 --> 00:05:24,154
Then, on November ninth, a week before the vote, another massacre.

92
00:05:24,204 --> 00:05:28,050
In the prison in Machala, at least thirty one inmates were killed.

93
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:30,945
Chapter 5. November tenth.

94
00:05:33,075 --> 00:05:36,891
The next day, Monday, November tenth, the transfer began.

95
00:05:37,641 --> 00:05:39,526
According to Ecuadorian media,

96
00:05:39,576 --> 00:05:44,236
around four hundred police and soldiers surrounded the area. Helicopters,

97
00:05:44,286 --> 00:05:49,526
military trucks and buses moved prisoners from several prisons, including La Roca,

98
00:05:49,576 --> 00:05:53,655
Machala, Esmeraldas and Guayaquil. Some were flown to the coast,

99
00:05:53,705 --> 00:05:55,461
then driven to the new site.

100
00:05:56,211 --> 00:05:58,510
There was no ceremony. No ribbon cutting.

101
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,630
According to the news site Primicias,

102
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:03,838
the government handled the whole operation with secrecy.

103
00:06:05,188 --> 00:06:07,798
That evening, Noboa posted on social media.

104
00:06:07,848 --> 00:06:11,448
The first three hundred most dangerous prisoners, he wrote,

105
00:06:11,498 --> 00:06:15,779
had been transferred to El Encuentro. He shared photos of the inmates,

106
00:06:15,829 --> 00:06:17,635
heads shaved, sitting in rows.

107
00:06:17,685 --> 00:06:19,862
And he added a line that went viral.

108
00:06:19,912 --> 00:06:23,698
Ya empezarán con la quejadera. Now the complaining will start.

109
00:06:24,448 --> 00:06:29,663
The images looked deliberately like El Salvador. Shaved heads. Rows of men.

110
00:06:29,713 --> 00:06:32,331
A message to the gangs, and to voters.

111
00:06:33,081 --> 00:06:37,058
Among the three hundred was one name nobody expected. Jorge Glas,

112
00:06:37,108 --> 00:06:41,333
a former vice president of Ecuador, serving sentences for corruption.

113
00:06:41,383 --> 00:06:44,493
The interior minister said he would be safer there.

114
00:06:45,243 --> 00:06:49,990
The interior minister also explained why so many prisoners came from La Roca,

115
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:53,790
a high security prison in Guayaquil. The government, he said,

116
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,531
planned works there. So La Roca was emptied,

117
00:06:56,581 --> 00:07:00,393
and its prisoners sent to a prison that was still being built.

118
00:07:01,363 --> 00:07:03,776
Chapter 6. Thirty five percent.

119
00:07:05,906 --> 00:07:08,776
On November twelfth, two days after the transfer,

120
00:07:08,826 --> 00:07:10,505
Noboa gave a radio interview.

121
00:07:10,555 --> 00:07:13,067
He was asked about the state of the prison.

122
00:07:13,817 --> 00:07:16,440
His answer was surprising. The prison, he said,

123
00:07:16,490 --> 00:07:19,169
was about thirty five to forty percent finished.

124
00:07:19,219 --> 00:07:22,467
He expected it to reach eighty percent in about two weeks,

125
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:25,310
and the last pavilion to be done in about a month.

126
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:31,119
So three hundred of the most dangerous men in the country had been moved into a building that was,

127
00:07:31,169 --> 00:07:34,091
by the president's own estimate, less than half complete.

128
00:07:35,641 --> 00:07:37,482
Why? Noboa gave a direct answer.

129
00:07:37,532 --> 00:07:41,146
He said there were plans to cause chaos around the referendum.

130
00:07:41,196 --> 00:07:43,391
They wanted to cause trouble, he said,

131
00:07:43,441 --> 00:07:45,519
and kill as many people as possible.

132
00:07:45,569 --> 00:07:47,055
We had to get ahead of it.

133
00:07:47,805 --> 00:07:49,347
Critics saw it differently.

134
00:07:49,397 --> 00:07:54,063
Electoral law in Ecuador limits the promotion of public works during a campaign.

135
00:07:54,113 --> 00:07:57,895
And the president had just shown the country his new mega prison,

136
00:07:57,945 --> 00:07:59,015
days before a vote.

137
00:07:59,065 --> 00:08:03,967
One opposition lawmaker said he was refused entry when he tried to inspect the site.

138
00:08:04,717 --> 00:08:09,756
In the same interview, Noboa announced something even bigger. Another prison,

139
00:08:09,806 --> 00:08:15,177
near El Encuentro, for around fifteen thousand inmates. Medium security this time.

140
00:08:15,927 --> 00:08:18,471
On November sixteenth, Ecuadorians voted.

141
00:08:18,521 --> 00:08:21,762
And they rejected all of the government's proposals.

142
00:08:21,812 --> 00:08:25,243
Around sixty percent said no to foreign military bases.

143
00:08:25,293 --> 00:08:28,533
It was Noboa's first major defeat at the ballot box.

144
00:08:28,583 --> 00:08:32,647
But El Encuentro was already open, and already full of prisoners.

145
00:08:34,217 --> 00:08:35,956
Chapter 7. Inside.

146
00:08:38,086 --> 00:08:41,896
For weeks, no journalists were allowed in. In early December,

147
00:08:41,946 --> 00:08:46,767
rumors spread online that prisoners had escaped. The prison agency denied it.

148
00:08:46,817 --> 00:08:50,374
The interior minister posted the first photo from inside,

149
00:08:50,424 --> 00:08:53,158
with a short caption. Photo kills the story.

150
00:08:53,908 --> 00:08:57,390
In February twenty twenty six, three months after the opening,

151
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:00,808
television reporters were finally allowed to visit. By then,

152
00:09:00,858 --> 00:09:03,542
the prison held six hundred and thirty five men.

153
00:09:04,292 --> 00:09:08,897
Each module has forty cells. Four prisoners per cell. Metal bunks,

154
00:09:08,947 --> 00:09:13,341
a desk, a toilet and a metal shower. Fire resistant mattresses.

155
00:09:13,391 --> 00:09:17,996
No switches. No sockets. Only small ventilation holes in the mesh.

156
00:09:18,746 --> 00:09:22,665
The corridors are lit at all times. When food or medicine arrives,

157
00:09:22,715 --> 00:09:25,552
a sound is played. Prisoners must face the wall,

158
00:09:25,602 --> 00:09:28,800
with their hands on their heads, until a second sound.

159
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:33,958
Unlike in Ecuador's other prisons, inmates are not separated by gang.

160
00:09:34,008 --> 00:09:37,512
Members of rival groups are mixed together, on purpose.

161
00:09:38,262 --> 00:09:42,256
New arrivals are held in small cells, shaved, and given new clothes.

162
00:09:42,306 --> 00:09:46,716
The prison blocks mobile and internet signals. According to the government,

163
00:09:46,766 --> 00:09:49,511
around five hundred drones have been shot down.

164
00:09:50,261 --> 00:09:52,524
Getting in takes five security steps.

165
00:09:52,574 --> 00:09:55,336
A sixth step exists for visitors and lawyers.

166
00:09:55,386 --> 00:09:59,212
But according to the government, that, quote, will not happen.

167
00:10:00,182 --> 00:10:02,118
Chapter 8. Not CECOT.

168
00:10:04,248 --> 00:10:06,514
El Encuentro is often compared to CECOT.

169
00:10:06,564 --> 00:10:08,310
But the two are very different.

170
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:13,306
CECOT, in El Salvador, has an official capacity of forty thousand.

171
00:10:13,356 --> 00:10:17,929
El Encuentro was designed for about eight hundred. Fifty times smaller.

172
00:10:18,679 --> 00:10:21,199
At CECOT, according to a BBC analysis,

173
00:10:21,249 --> 00:10:25,528
each cell holds on average around one hundred and fifty six men.

174
00:10:25,578 --> 00:10:28,301
At El Encuentro, it is four men per cell.

175
00:10:29,051 --> 00:10:32,387
CECOT was presented to the world as a finished building.

176
00:10:32,437 --> 00:10:35,833
El Encuentro received its first prisoners quietly, while,

177
00:10:35,883 --> 00:10:39,823
by the president's own account, it was still less than half built.

178
00:10:40,573 --> 00:10:45,494
What they share is the philosophy. Shaved heads. Total control of movement.

179
00:10:45,544 --> 00:10:47,747
No contact with the outside world.

180
00:10:47,797 --> 00:10:51,326
And a message aimed as much at voters as at the gangs.

181
00:10:52,296 --> 00:10:53,919
Chapter 9. The cost.

182
00:10:56,049 --> 00:10:58,472
In twenty twenty six, the deaths began.

183
00:10:59,822 --> 00:11:00,977
On April thirteenth,

184
00:11:01,027 --> 00:11:04,954
a prisoner died in hospital from complications of a liver disease.

185
00:11:05,004 --> 00:11:11,824
He had been convicted of terrorism for the armed attack on the TC television studio in January twenty twenty four.

186
00:11:12,574 --> 00:11:15,153
On May fifteenth, another prisoner died.

187
00:11:15,203 --> 00:11:19,359
The government called it a natural death. On May twenty seventh,

188
00:11:19,409 --> 00:11:23,303
a third. Again, the official explanation was natural causes.

189
00:11:24,053 --> 00:11:28,587
There were reports of tuberculosis. The government denied there was an outbreak.

190
00:11:28,637 --> 00:11:30,535
But over a holiday weekend in May,

191
00:11:30,585 --> 00:11:34,489
thirty one prisoners were moved out of El Encuentro, as a precaution.

192
00:11:35,239 --> 00:11:39,973
And in October twenty twenty six, the prison agency confirmed another death,

193
00:11:40,023 --> 00:11:44,632
after surgery in a hospital in Guayaquil. By then, according to Primicias,

194
00:11:44,682 --> 00:11:50,864
families and lawyers had filed around seventy legal complaints over alleged abuse and malnutrition.

195
00:11:51,614 --> 00:11:53,821
The government says the prison is secure,

196
00:11:53,871 --> 00:11:56,023
and that prisoners receive medical care.

197
00:11:56,073 --> 00:11:59,106
Critics say a prison that opened before it was finished,

198
00:11:59,156 --> 00:12:02,630
in a place without basic services, was always going to struggle.

199
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:05,316
Chapter 10. Did it work?

200
00:12:07,446 --> 00:12:10,147
So, did El Encuentro make Ecuador safer?

201
00:12:10,897 --> 00:12:11,853
It is hard to say.

202
00:12:11,903 --> 00:12:17,048
The prison holds only a few hundred of the country's roughly thirty eight thousand prisoners.

203
00:12:17,098 --> 00:12:21,405
Violence in other prisons has continued. Ecuador's prisons remain overcrowded.

204
00:12:22,155 --> 00:12:26,232
What El Encuentro did change was the message. For the first time,

205
00:12:26,282 --> 00:12:30,551
Ecuador could show the world its own version of CECOT. Shaved heads.

206
00:12:30,601 --> 00:12:33,662
Orange uniforms. A wall in the middle of nowhere.

207
00:12:34,412 --> 00:12:36,108
But CECOT took years to build,

208
00:12:36,158 --> 00:12:39,076
and was finished before the first prisoner arrived.

209
00:12:39,126 --> 00:12:42,394
El Encuentro was opened in a hurry, a week before a vote,

210
00:12:42,444 --> 00:12:43,965
at less than half complete.

211
00:12:44,715 --> 00:12:48,759
Maybe the government was right, and moving those men quickly saved lives.

212
00:12:48,809 --> 00:12:52,909
Or maybe the rush created new problems that are only now becoming visible.

213
00:12:53,659 --> 00:12:54,839
So what do you think?

214
00:12:54,889 --> 00:12:58,703
Was opening an unfinished prison a smart move, or a dangerous one?

215
00:12:58,753 --> 00:13:00,108
Tell us in the comments.

216
00:13:00,158 --> 00:13:02,391
And to see the prison that inspired it,

217
00:13:02,441 --> 00:13:05,026
watch our story about CECOT, on this channel.
